from the white house it was an  announcement designed to surprise  to make a president facing a tough  election look like the broker  the deal maker and it does because on  the face of it the abraham accord is a  rabbit  out of the hat a significant moment in  the story of the middle east peace  process  just a few moments ago i hosted a very  special call with two friends  prime minister benjamin netanyahu of  israel  and crown prince mohammed bin zaid  of the united arab emirates where they  agreed to finalize a historical peace  agreement  everybody said this would be impossible  in israel it's a win too for prime  minister netanyahu  this is the greatest advancements toward  peace between israel and the arab world  in the last 26 years and it marks the  third formal peace between  israel and an arab nation the suggestion  is he will now suspend plans to annex  parts of the palestinian west bank  but in return he's got a deal with the  uae and a strengthened axis against  common arab-israeli enemy  iran it is more than a quarter of a  century since an arab country created  diplomatic ties with israel  it was bill clinton who bought king  hussein of jordan together with yitzhak  rabin of israel  the only other arab nation with  diplomatic ties with the jewish state  is egypt that deal signed back in 1979.  the foreign minister of the third arab  country now to make this huge diplomatic  shift  spoke to sky news this is a win-win deal  it's a win-win deal for peace  in the region and it brings actually  a ray of hope in a very difficult time  in our region  not known really for good times anyway  in jerusalem there is now the prospect  of gulf arab tourists wandering the old  city  visiting the al-aqsa mosque for the  first time for commerce for tourism for  aviation for geopolitics this is  certainly a crucial juncture  in the long road to middle east peace  but on the other side of israel's  security wall in the west bank there is  a deep sense of betrayal  until now any arab-israeli diplomatic  deal was largely dependent on the  emergence of a palestinian state  and yet these people are no closer to  having that state they remain occupied  replying on twitter to the abu dhabi  crown prince's announcement of the deal  top palestinian diplomat hanan ashwari  wrote  may you never experience the agony of  having your country stolen  may you never feel the pain of living in  captivity under occupation  may you never witness the demolition of  your home or murder of your loved ones  she concluded may you never be sold out  by your friends  the geopolitical reality though now  seems to be that trade commerce  and a strengthened axis against iran now  trumps the palestinian cause  the question now is how many other arab  nations will follow  mark stone sky news in jerusalem
 

